Leather does have the advantage that you're sure to be able to
get them, fairly early.

> > Except that:
> >     for combat wombat: leather < dexterity < power
> >     for a spellcaster: power < leather < dexterity

> > In both cases, the difference between power and the others
> > is significant; the difference between dexterity and leather
> > is usually fairly negligeable.  (And of course, you don't
> > want fumbling, period.)

> I disagree with the value of power gloves to combat wombats.

They increase damage.

> Once strength is maxed

Typically only happens later in the game.  And depends on race;
I see no reason to stick with power later in the game, if you're
human or a dwarf.  For a gnome or an orc, it's more useful, and
for an elf (but are there any elvin combat wombats), it can make
a big difference.

Early in the game, before strength is max'ed out, the increased
damage can also be an important factor.

Dexterity also plays a much smaller role later in the game;
beyond a certain point, you're hitting 100% anyway (and
dexterity only affects to hit, not damage).  Earlier in the
game, improving your to hit percentage is useful, but maximizing
luck helps here, and of course, unlike power, the effect of
dexterity depends on the enchantment---early in the game, before
you've got your scrolls of enchantment, you don't necessarily
get anything from them.

> they only help for a valk who wishes to throw Mojo.  The
> damage when Mojo's return flight is not caught is enough that
> I rarely wish to throw it.  My version goes:

> for combat wombat leather < power < dexterity

> With power and dexterity preference very ambiguous earlier in
> the game then growing more pronounced as the game progresses.

What does dexterity add once you've got your to hit to 100%?

> for spell caster power < leather < dexterity

> With power and leather preference very ambiguous earlier in
> the game but as soon as dexterity comes available the
> difference is large enough that power or leather get dropped.

> Increased dexterity adds to the to-hit role but maxed out luck
> and skill and enchantment see to it that swings/shots never
> miss so that feature is redundant.  But it also helps for
> doors and traps and such if I understand dexterity correctly.
> It's a subtle but useful advantage.

Luck affects these as well.  Dexerity can offset some problems
with luck, but basically:

 -- If I can cast spells, and count on doing so, I don't use
    power.  Otherwise, I'll go with power.

 -- Between dexterity and leather, I'll take dexterity, but I've
    never felt the difference worth making any additional effort
    to get them---I'd never waste a wish on dexterity, even if
    all I had were leather.  And if I've already enchanted my
    leather, I won't even bother picking up dexterity.

> The choice among head gear is more complex.  A spell caster
> may want an elven leather helmet until corun-mumble or
> brilliance is found but a character without infravision
> benefits from ESP enough that it remains useful throughout.  A
> combat wombat might want to use a drawvish iron helmet early
> on but a similar late tradeoff between brilliance and ESP
> happens.  With helmets, visored have an advantage that can
> push the difference one way or another.

Most of the other armor are more complex.  I'll usually go with
brilliance, since once enchanted, it means more spells can be
successfully cast, and even my combat wombats like to use spells
from time to time (curse sickness, after an encounter with
pestilence, for example).  But I've also ascended characters
who've never successfully cast a spell.

